1
00:00:03,461 --> 00:00:06,757
On the small rooftop garden,
the wind blows again today.

2
00:00:06,762 --> 00:00:09,315
The tomatoes have not turned red yet.

3
00:00:09,711 --> 00:00:12,635
Well then, let's begin today's story.

4
00:00:21,021 --> 00:00:22,478
Sensei!

5
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:23,690
You're late, I've been waiting!

6
00:00:23,748 --> 00:00:25,434
Yeah, sorry, sorry.

7
00:00:25,434 --> 00:00:28,388
I took the long way on my morning walk.

8
00:00:28,782 --> 00:00:31,001
Never mind that. What's in the paper bag?

9
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:32,374
Melon bread.

10
00:00:32,575 --> 00:00:33,834
I knew it!

11
00:00:34,036 --> 00:00:36,086
Forget the bread, Sensei!

12
00:00:37,819 --> 00:00:39,880
The tomato's turned red!

13
00:00:59,372 --> 00:01:02,586
The top one, look. It's
fully red all over.

14
00:01:02,659 --> 00:01:03,834
Really?

15
00:01:03,886 --> 00:01:06,674
It was still green yesterday, but
this morning it was bright red.

16
00:01:06,674 --> 00:01:07,936
I almost screamed, honestly.

17
00:01:07,945 --> 00:01:09,221
Can you believe it?

18
00:01:11,032 --> 00:01:12,515
Have you been bouncing
like that the whole time?

19
00:01:12,515 --> 00:01:14,860
I can't keep still when
something this big happens!

20
00:01:14,969 --> 00:01:15,762
I see.

21
00:01:15,851 --> 00:01:16,750
It took a long while
for the fruit to come.

22
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:17,883
Waiting is always the
hardest part, isn't it.

23
00:01:17,883 --> 00:01:19,088
Mm, I see.

24
00:01:19,112 --> 00:01:23,352
Wait, I'll go get everyone!
Stay right here and watch!

25
00:01:23,421 --> 00:01:24,578
Sensei, hurry!

26
00:02:58,106 --> 00:03:01,869
I'm counting every one,
so nobody touch anything.

27
00:03:02,964 --> 00:03:04,197
Okay!

28
00:03:05,532 --> 00:03:07,361
Twelve green, three yellow.

29
00:03:07,361 --> 00:03:08,637
What about the red ones?

30
00:03:08,692 --> 00:03:11,991
Red... just one, and I've
checked it three times.

31
00:03:13,143 --> 00:03:15,360
One? You've got to be joking.

32
00:03:15,639 --> 00:03:17,391
It's not a joke! Look, over there!

33
00:03:17,464 --> 00:03:18,914
...It's tiny.

34
00:03:21,193 --> 00:03:21,998
It's small, but look at that deep red.

35
00:03:21,998 --> 00:03:23,013
Technically, that's the ideal ripeness.

36
00:03:23,091 --> 00:03:24,335
That's a deep red.

37
00:03:24,748 --> 00:03:25,718
Can we touch it?

38
00:03:25,718 --> 00:03:27,743
No touching it, not even a finger.

39
00:03:27,802 --> 00:03:28,822
Aw...

40
00:03:28,905 --> 00:03:31,485
This is a miracle. Take your hands off it.

41
00:03:31,655 --> 00:03:33,200
A miracle? That's a bit much.

42
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:35,969
It's not an exaggeration.
Three weeks to get one fruit.

43
00:03:35,969 --> 00:03:37,182
That's not nothing.

44
00:03:40,305 --> 00:03:42,203
Waiting is part of the harvest too.

45
00:04:02,354 --> 00:04:03,733
There it is, sensei's wisdom.

46
00:04:03,846 --> 00:04:05,187
Is it wisdom?

47
00:04:07,938 --> 00:04:09,531
Anyway, did you bring the melon bread?

48
00:04:09,625 --> 00:04:11,170
Sensei, now's not the time!

49
00:04:11,262 --> 00:04:12,447
I'm taking a photo.

50
00:04:12,493 --> 00:04:14,916
Hold on, I need to fix the
angle of the light first.

51
00:04:15,014 --> 00:04:15,875
Hurry up, then.

52
00:04:15,892 --> 00:04:17,516
I'm measuring it.

53
00:04:17,776 --> 00:04:19,235
Three centimeters across, eighteen grams.

54
00:04:19,235 --> 00:04:20,951
Technically, that's small for a tomato.

55
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:25,329
You actually weighed it?

56
00:04:25,365 --> 00:04:26,487
Of course.

57
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:31,518
I've already got a name for it!

58
00:04:31,518 --> 00:04:32,960
It's too early for names.

59
00:04:33,033 --> 00:04:34,676
Names matter, you know.

60
00:04:35,131 --> 00:04:38,184
A miracle has to be recorded
properly before anyone touches it.

61
00:04:43,667 --> 00:04:45,079
I'll be the one who takes it to him.

62
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:46,211
No.

63
00:04:46,250 --> 00:04:47,439
Excuse me?

64
00:04:47,791 --> 00:04:48,908
I'm the president. That makes it my call.

65
00:04:49,035 --> 00:04:51,291
I'm the one who let it die. This is on me.

66
00:04:51,364 --> 00:04:52,527
That was last year.

67
00:04:52,527 --> 00:04:54,778
Stop dragging it into this.

68
00:04:57,236 --> 00:04:58,235
I'm not dragging anything.

69
00:04:58,251 --> 00:04:59,695
Liar.

70
00:04:59,793 --> 00:05:02,399
I skipped watering for a
week. Nobody else did that.

71
00:05:02,503 --> 00:05:03,376
I know. Everyone knows.

72
00:05:03,376 --> 00:05:05,884
Then I should be the one apologizing
to him. That's only fair.

73
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,559
You can apologize once the tomato's safe.

74
00:05:08,559 --> 00:05:09,469
Not before.

75
00:05:09,569 --> 00:05:10,652
Fine. Whatever.

76
00:05:10,686 --> 00:05:12,794
Don't decide that on your own.

77
00:05:13,090 --> 00:05:14,873
I'm not convinced at all.

78
00:05:14,873 --> 00:05:16,293
Then what do you want?

79
00:05:16,328 --> 00:05:17,441
I'll do the talking.

80
00:05:17,839 --> 00:05:18,795
Impossible.

81
00:05:18,849 --> 00:05:20,368
I can talk just fine, you know.

82
00:05:20,558 --> 00:05:22,352
You stood there glaring at the
principal for twenty minutes last time.

83
00:05:22,352 --> 00:05:23,530
You didn't say a word.

84
00:05:23,641 --> 00:05:25,137
I was thinking, that's all.

85
00:05:25,201 --> 00:05:27,113
You looked like you wanted to hit someone.

86
00:05:29,087 --> 00:05:30,462
Shut up.

87
00:05:30,979 --> 00:05:32,051
Wait!

88
00:05:32,073 --> 00:05:33,548
See? That look right there.

89
00:05:33,548 --> 00:05:34,649
I'm not looking at you.

90
00:05:34,699 --> 00:05:35,558
You clearly are.

91
00:05:35,679 --> 00:05:36,530
I see.

92
00:05:36,530 --> 00:05:38,503
What do you mean, you see?

93
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:39,217
Why don't we both go?

94
00:05:39,217 --> 00:05:40,730
Then nobody gets blamed alone.

95
00:05:40,886 --> 00:05:42,217
That's cheating.

96
00:05:42,898 --> 00:05:44,203
I decided that on the spot.

97
00:05:44,217 --> 00:05:45,319
Okay. Fine.

98
00:05:54,757 --> 00:05:55,980
Both of us, then. That works.

99
00:06:04,901 --> 00:06:07,195
Please submit the report.

100
00:06:07,195 --> 00:06:08,120
Yes! Right now!

101
00:06:08,223 --> 00:06:09,114
Wait, it isn't finished yet.

102
00:06:09,114 --> 00:06:09,804
Give us one minute.

103
00:06:09,833 --> 00:06:12,979
I don't accept unfinished reports.

104
00:06:13,497 --> 00:06:15,484
Then I'll explain it out loud.

105
00:06:15,585 --> 00:06:16,633
That will do.

106
00:06:17,127 --> 00:06:20,233
Okay. First, the biggest
problem was aphids.

107
00:06:20,335 --> 00:06:25,209
Three weeks ago they covered the leaf
undersides. Over a hundred of them.

108
00:06:25,271 --> 00:06:28,171
Technically, it was a
hundred and forty-two.

109
00:06:28,259 --> 00:06:30,372
Who cares about the exact number?

110
00:06:30,643 --> 00:06:31,677
This is the important part.

111
00:06:31,705 --> 00:06:33,602
We didn't use pesticide.

112
00:06:34,641 --> 00:06:35,501
We picked them off by hand

113
00:06:35,501 --> 00:06:37,152
and washed the plants in soapy water.

114
00:06:37,347 --> 00:06:39,254
Every morning, starting at six.

115
00:06:40,899 --> 00:06:42,676
And?

116
00:06:42,930 --> 00:06:48,661
For the grafts we used two
rootstocks, in case one got sick.

117
00:06:48,673 --> 00:06:50,479
We cut the joints at a slant

118
00:06:50,479 --> 00:06:52,292
and matched them exactly.

119
00:06:52,407 --> 00:06:54,178
The joints fit perfectly!

120
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:55,382
Quiet.

121
00:06:55,733 --> 00:06:57,434
Both of them took.

122
00:06:59,277 --> 00:07:00,082
Wow!

123
00:07:00,152 --> 00:07:01,678
One of them died anyway.

124
00:07:01,678 --> 00:07:04,218
But the other one bore fruit.

125
00:07:07,602 --> 00:07:08,742
I see.

126
00:07:08,843 --> 00:07:11,574
That's the full report. Any questions?

127
00:07:31,494 --> 00:07:32,572
There are gaps.

128
00:07:32,572 --> 00:07:33,786
I know.

129
00:07:34,410 --> 00:07:39,112
Long reports wear a person
out. Don't they, Principal?

130
00:07:39,211 --> 00:07:41,346
Miyashita-sensei, be quiet.

131
00:07:41,424 --> 00:07:43,523
Now, show me the tomato.

132
00:07:43,588 --> 00:07:44,577
Yes.

133
00:07:44,577 --> 00:07:46,492
Please don't touch it, okay?

134
00:07:52,025 --> 00:07:53,508
Then I'll try it.

135
00:07:56,344 --> 00:07:57,294
Go ahead.

136
00:07:57,392 --> 00:07:58,595
The skin is thin.

137
00:07:58,645 --> 00:08:00,303
You can tell from the skin?!

138
00:08:00,303 --> 00:08:01,732
Be quiet.

139
00:08:02,162 --> 00:08:03,473
...

140
00:08:26,805 --> 00:08:28,224
It's sweet.

141
00:08:29,081 --> 00:08:30,766
The sourness comes after.

142
00:08:30,773 --> 00:08:36,049
Technically, it's 8.2 Brix, but
taste doesn't measure well.

143
00:08:36,095 --> 00:08:36,929
Sweet, right?

144
00:08:37,002 --> 00:08:38,015
It tastes like the ones I ate as a child.

145
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:38,758
Those are hard to find now.

146
00:08:38,815 --> 00:08:41,402
Did the principal just smile?

147
00:08:41,985 --> 00:08:42,999
I'm not smiling.

148
00:08:42,999 --> 00:08:44,025
Principal.

149
00:08:44,054 --> 00:08:45,042
I have one question.

150
00:08:45,131 --> 00:08:46,398
Why do you need the roof?

151
00:08:46,398 --> 00:08:47,863
Give me an actual reason.

152
00:08:47,953 --> 00:08:48,796
...

153
00:08:48,851 --> 00:08:50,788
Well, the reason is...

154
00:08:51,464 --> 00:08:52,780
Wait.

155
00:08:52,957 --> 00:08:54,360
There are tomatoes on the roof!

156
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:54,993
Tons of them!

157
00:08:55,054 --> 00:08:56,358
That's not an answer.

158
00:08:56,474 --> 00:09:00,649
Even if we answer, the
rules still win, right?

159
00:09:00,972 --> 00:09:02,342
A rule is a rule.

160
00:09:02,354 --> 00:09:05,017
I think you already know
the answer, Principal.

161
00:09:05,072 --> 00:09:06,062
Miyashita-sensei.

162
00:09:06,135 --> 00:09:07,330
Yes, sorry.

163
00:09:07,943 --> 00:09:10,279
We want to grow things here. That's all.

164
00:09:10,337 --> 00:09:12,929
If it's only growing, pots will do.

165
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:19,260
Pots aren't enough. It has to be the roof.

166
00:09:19,303 --> 00:09:21,779
The sunlight and the wind
are different up here.

167
00:09:32,150 --> 00:09:34,478
What's with that face?

168
00:09:34,674 --> 00:09:36,192
It's not a big story or anything.

169
00:09:36,252 --> 00:09:37,796
My grandfather grew vegetables up here.

170
00:09:37,796 --> 00:09:39,681
Right on this corner, actually.

171
00:09:39,784 --> 00:09:40,841
Huh?

172
00:09:40,958 --> 00:09:45,891
He farmed behind the school. When they
built it up, every field was gone.

173
00:09:45,918 --> 00:09:46,985
Yeah.

174
00:09:47,086 --> 00:09:49,962
I think he never got over that.

175
00:09:49,975 --> 00:09:52,795
The last time he came up here was
the spring I started first year.

176
00:09:53,009 --> 00:09:56,861
His legs were already weak, but he wouldn't
listen. He insisted on planting tomatoes.

177
00:09:56,907 --> 00:10:02,024
I helped. He taught me
how to set every stake.

178
00:10:02,137 --> 00:10:03,406
Yeah.

179
00:10:05,529 --> 00:10:08,091
That summer, he was
admitted to the hospital.

180
00:10:08,091 --> 00:10:09,917
By autumn, he wasn't coming back.

181
00:10:10,031 --> 00:10:14,861
I was the one who had to harvest what
was left. But it was all ruined.

182
00:10:15,211 --> 00:10:17,432
Blight. It all rotted.

183
00:10:17,949 --> 00:10:19,395
Wait, so that means...

184
00:10:20,050 --> 00:10:22,293
Yes. I killed them. It was my fault.

185
00:10:22,826 --> 00:10:24,087
So I quit.

186
00:10:27,221 --> 00:10:28,529
I ran away, that's all.

187
00:10:28,561 --> 00:10:29,954
Why?

188
00:10:30,310 --> 00:10:32,707
Why didn't you tell us before?

189
00:10:32,707 --> 00:10:34,765
There was no reason to.

190
00:10:34,848 --> 00:10:38,257
Joking it off was easier.

191
00:10:59,982 --> 00:11:02,726
But when I come up here,
I can actually breathe.

192
00:11:02,783 --> 00:11:06,296
I don't belong in the
classroom or at home.

193
00:11:06,370 --> 00:11:08,123
This is the only place
I feel like I belong.

194
00:11:08,123 --> 00:11:09,578
It's the only place that's mine.

195
00:11:09,628 --> 00:11:11,970
I know how embarrassing that sounds.

196
00:11:11,981 --> 00:11:13,124
Go ahead, laugh.

197
00:11:13,627 --> 00:11:14,840
Not laughing.

198
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:15,968
Keep going.

199
00:11:16,006 --> 00:11:19,591
Technically, that's the most
accurate thing you've said all week.

200
00:11:21,953 --> 00:11:22,874
Shut up.

201
00:11:22,881 --> 00:11:24,583
I'm not crying!

202
00:11:27,835 --> 00:11:30,162
Plants grow in one place too, you know.

203
00:11:30,203 --> 00:11:31,464
Owada.

204
00:11:32,947 --> 00:11:37,173
You're allowed to stay here,
Owada. We're keeping this roof.

205
00:11:51,790 --> 00:11:54,941
Right on time. Everyone, take a seat.

206
00:11:54,977 --> 00:11:56,256
Yes!

207
00:11:56,290 --> 00:11:59,505
Today we reach a decision
about the rooftop.

208
00:11:59,637 --> 00:12:01,811
Are we deciding today?

209
00:12:02,828 --> 00:12:04,099
We are. With conditions.

210
00:12:04,099 --> 00:12:05,206
Listen carefully.

211
00:12:05,243 --> 00:12:07,786
Could you put the conditions in numbers?

212
00:12:08,276 --> 00:12:09,450
A six-month trial.

213
00:12:09,450 --> 00:12:10,927
It starts now.

214
00:12:12,299 --> 00:12:13,363
Six months.

215
00:12:13,380 --> 00:12:16,549
Half the roof stays a garden.

216
00:12:19,782 --> 00:12:21,283
Wait, half?!

217
00:12:22,737 --> 00:12:25,178
Half of it stays?

218
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:29,987
The other half goes to solar
panels. Those are the terms.

219
00:12:30,096 --> 00:12:34,786
Technically, half the roof might
still produce enough power.

220
00:12:34,786 --> 00:12:38,205
Numbers come later. Today
we accept the terms.

221
00:12:38,253 --> 00:12:40,316
There it is. The principal's "later."

222
00:12:40,615 --> 00:12:43,031
Owada. Watch your mouth.

223
00:12:43,117 --> 00:12:44,554
Sorry.

224
00:12:44,633 --> 00:12:47,407
Come on. Half sounds fine to me.

225
00:12:47,587 --> 00:12:51,429
Miyashita-sensei. Will
you sign as advisor?

226
00:12:53,813 --> 00:12:54,375
I'll sign it.

227
00:12:54,375 --> 00:12:55,448
Just one small condition.

228
00:12:55,501 --> 00:12:56,742
One condition.

229
00:12:56,779 --> 00:12:57,666
Go on.

230
00:12:57,693 --> 00:13:00,801
The club pays for the melon
bread. One for everyone.

231
00:13:00,846 --> 00:13:02,747
Sensei, that's not a condition!

232
00:13:02,762 --> 00:13:04,150
It's important.

233
00:13:04,429 --> 00:13:07,595
...Fine. I'll find the budget somehow.

234
00:13:08,506 --> 00:13:09,698
Yay!

235
00:13:10,934 --> 00:13:13,694
Sir, is that an official resolution?

236
00:13:16,585 --> 00:13:17,769
Put it in the minutes.

237
00:13:17,769 --> 00:13:21,481
We'll show results. I promise.

238
00:13:21,518 --> 00:13:24,714
I'll admit the taste. Now show me numbers.

239
00:13:37,606 --> 00:13:39,689
You like the tomatoes, don't you, sir?

240
00:13:40,836 --> 00:13:42,933
That's not on the agenda.

241
00:13:50,460 --> 00:13:54,289
Technically, we only have
six months of protection.

242
00:13:54,348 --> 00:13:55,674
Yeah.

243
00:13:56,182 --> 00:13:58,553
What happens after that?

244
00:13:58,610 --> 00:14:01,285
Thinking about it won't help.
Right now is what counts.

245
00:14:02,562 --> 00:14:05,562
But the principal's offer has
too many conditions, doesn't it?

246
00:14:05,562 --> 00:14:06,523
It does.

247
00:14:06,588 --> 00:14:09,860
The fine print says if the yield
drops, it's cancelled right away.

248
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:11,702
Are you saying it's a trap?

249
00:14:11,802 --> 00:14:14,150
I don't know. That's why it scares me.

250
00:14:14,851 --> 00:14:16,106
Wait.

251
00:14:16,555 --> 00:14:17,428
What?

252
00:14:17,428 --> 00:14:20,293
If there are conditions, it keeps
going as long as we meet them.

253
00:14:20,293 --> 00:14:21,458
That's the deal, right?

254
00:14:21,516 --> 00:14:23,855
...That's true, but...

255
00:14:24,058 --> 00:14:28,550
Technically, it's a fair deal,
as long as we keep producing.

256
00:14:29,410 --> 00:14:30,809
We will.

257
00:14:34,219 --> 00:14:37,903
We weigh and measure the sugar every week.

258
00:14:37,903 --> 00:14:40,228
We will. I'll have Tomoe help too.

259
00:14:50,290 --> 00:14:53,467
Tomoe falls asleep the
second she sees numbers.

260
00:14:53,555 --> 00:14:54,812
Then she's in charge of naming things.

261
00:14:54,812 --> 00:14:56,822
Haha, that she's good at.

262
00:15:00,015 --> 00:15:01,550
So, the next seedlings. What do we do?

263
00:15:03,391 --> 00:15:07,227
We still have three
rootstocks for grafting.

264
00:15:07,333 --> 00:15:10,684
One goes on the plant with the red fruit.

265
00:15:10,742 --> 00:15:12,729
What if disease shows up?

266
00:15:12,729 --> 00:15:13,928
We split the leaves and grow them apart.

267
00:15:13,928 --> 00:15:14,770
Isolated, so it can't spread.

268
00:15:14,785 --> 00:15:17,869
We should set a fixed watering time too.

269
00:15:17,924 --> 00:15:18,931
Six in the morning.

270
00:15:18,931 --> 00:15:20,460
Evenings, before sunset.

271
00:15:20,580 --> 00:15:22,224
Water once the heat has dropped.

272
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:24,819
Got it. What about aphids?

273
00:15:25,232 --> 00:15:25,937
Soap water first.

274
00:15:25,937 --> 00:15:27,186
If that fails, ladybugs.

275
00:15:27,694 --> 00:15:30,670
Ladybugs fly off if you
release them on a roof.

276
00:15:32,130 --> 00:15:34,306
Then we give them a reason to stay.

277
00:15:34,379 --> 00:15:35,761
That's extreme.

278
00:15:35,794 --> 00:15:38,416
Next harvest, every single one turns red.

279
00:15:41,819 --> 00:15:42,995
I've picked a name!

280
00:15:43,018 --> 00:15:44,017
Kurenai!

281
00:15:44,049 --> 00:15:45,200
Kurenai?

282
00:15:45,219 --> 00:15:46,420
It's red, so it's Kurenai.

283
00:15:46,861 --> 00:15:48,075
Isn't that a bit too simple?

284
00:15:48,586 --> 00:15:50,243
Simple is best!

285
00:15:50,454 --> 00:15:51,760
If we're going to name
it, let's think harder.

286
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:53,477
Something a little more dignified, maybe?

287
00:15:53,966 --> 00:15:55,991
Kurenai is what I came up with!

288
00:15:56,025 --> 00:15:57,084
Wait.

289
00:15:57,084 --> 00:16:00,071
Chef Kurenai. Sounds like
a sushi shop owner, right?

290
00:16:00,335 --> 00:16:01,842
Huh?

291
00:16:02,690 --> 00:16:04,544
It's not a shop owner! It's Kurenai!

292
00:16:04,638 --> 00:16:07,805
Technically, a tomato
isn't a sushi topping.

293
00:16:14,366 --> 00:16:16,454
So it's tomato sushi, then.

294
00:16:16,973 --> 00:16:18,888
Kurenai is Kurenai! It's not a tomato!

295
00:16:21,039 --> 00:16:21,962
I see.

296
00:16:22,083 --> 00:16:24,627
If we're keeping it, we should at least
be able to say it. Kurenai, okay?

297
00:16:24,672 --> 00:16:26,085
I can say it! Kurenai!

298
00:16:26,415 --> 00:16:27,439
I think Kurenai sounds nice.

299
00:16:27,439 --> 00:16:28,599
Very sushi-chef, though.

300
00:16:29,444 --> 00:16:32,416
You're just eating melon bread, Sensei.

301
00:16:32,574 --> 00:16:34,126
I'm thinking while I eat.

302
00:16:34,398 --> 00:16:35,695
What's the reasoning behind it?

303
00:16:35,729 --> 00:16:39,412
Because it was the first one
to turn red! I named it!

304
00:16:39,741 --> 00:16:40,998
Yeah, this one.

305
00:16:41,465 --> 00:16:42,946
That's confusing.

306
00:16:42,950 --> 00:16:44,994
Kurenai is this one, right here.

307
00:16:44,994 --> 00:16:47,923
Once you hear the name, you'll know it.

308
00:16:48,011 --> 00:16:49,884
Chef Kurenai of the sushi bar.

309
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:51,440
It's Kurenai! Kurenai! Kurenai!

310
00:16:52,233 --> 00:16:53,415
Stop it, already.

311
00:16:58,141 --> 00:17:00,561
Come over here a second, Owada.

312
00:17:00,654 --> 00:17:01,915
No.

313
00:17:04,013 --> 00:17:05,468
Oh, come on. Just humor me.

314
00:17:05,550 --> 00:17:07,188
What's with the wooden box?

315
00:17:07,197 --> 00:17:11,117
Rootstock and scion. The
cut decides everything.

316
00:17:11,117 --> 00:17:12,636
I've heard that a hundred times.

317
00:17:12,637 --> 00:17:15,171
Twenty degrees, with the blade laid low.

318
00:17:15,903 --> 00:17:17,658
Can't I just eyeball it?

319
00:17:17,689 --> 00:17:18,905
Eyeball it and you fail every time.

320
00:17:18,905 --> 00:17:20,391
Ask Koizumi. She'd tell you the same.

321
00:17:20,409 --> 00:17:23,157
If Koizumi heard that,
she'd bring you numbers.

322
00:17:23,157 --> 00:17:25,214
So you're the one who passes it on.

323
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:26,729
Huh?

324
00:17:26,812 --> 00:17:30,393
The cambium. The thin green
layer just under the bark.

325
00:17:30,393 --> 00:17:32,103
I can't see any green.

326
00:17:32,715 --> 00:17:34,384
Hold it up to the light and you'll see it.

327
00:17:34,489 --> 00:17:35,503
...There. I see it.

328
00:17:35,503 --> 00:17:37,553
You only need to line up this part.

329
00:17:39,090 --> 00:17:42,047
Won't it stick if the
whole cut doesn't match?

330
00:17:42,511 --> 00:17:44,191
One side lining up is enough.

331
00:17:44,191 --> 00:17:46,908
Plants are more forgiving
than you'd think.

332
00:17:47,021 --> 00:17:48,145
That's sloppy.

333
00:17:48,244 --> 00:17:50,242
A little sloppiness is just right.

334
00:17:50,757 --> 00:17:53,687
Coming from you, that's
not very convincing.

335
00:17:53,719 --> 00:17:55,929
Once it's matched, wrap it in tape.

336
00:17:56,047 --> 00:17:57,656
I know how to wrap it, at least.

337
00:17:57,656 --> 00:18:01,645
You knew how, but you never
did it. You're doing it today.

338
00:18:01,671 --> 00:18:03,652
...Fine. Got it.

339
00:18:05,480 --> 00:18:06,838
Owada.

340
00:18:06,907 --> 00:18:07,836
What?

341
00:18:07,859 --> 00:18:10,171
You never really left this club.

342
00:18:10,171 --> 00:18:10,717
I quit, remember?

343
00:18:10,717 --> 00:18:12,185
Don't make it sound like I came back.

344
00:18:12,933 --> 00:18:15,157
Come up to the roof again sometime.

345
00:18:15,157 --> 00:18:16,671
...Thanks.

346
00:18:19,442 --> 00:18:21,680
Look. The van's leaving through the gate.

347
00:18:22,707 --> 00:18:24,788
...It actually left.

348
00:18:24,868 --> 00:18:27,046
They said the proposal's been shelved.

349
00:18:27,224 --> 00:18:28,265
Shelved means on hold?

350
00:18:28,265 --> 00:18:29,282
So it's not dead?

351
00:18:29,321 --> 00:18:30,364
That's right. It isn't dead.

352
00:18:30,364 --> 00:18:31,585
It isn't safe yet, either.

353
00:18:31,698 --> 00:18:33,094
So did we win?

354
00:18:33,451 --> 00:18:35,386
I didn't say we won.

355
00:18:37,898 --> 00:18:39,825
Huh? That was a win!

356
00:18:39,995 --> 00:18:40,943
Yeah.

357
00:18:40,964 --> 00:18:44,156
...Well, today can count as a win.

358
00:19:00,127 --> 00:19:01,563
Sensei, the melon bread!

359
00:19:01,604 --> 00:19:03,446
I've already got the bag open.

360
00:19:03,753 --> 00:19:06,284
One each. I counted, so it's accurate.

361
00:19:06,392 --> 00:19:07,983
Sensei doesn't get one.

362
00:19:07,983 --> 00:19:10,758
Koizumi, isn't that a bit harsh?

363
00:19:11,221 --> 00:19:13,314
It's not harsh. It's arithmetic.

364
00:19:13,327 --> 00:19:15,669
You already ate half of yours, Sensei.

365
00:19:15,669 --> 00:19:18,437
That half was from before the meeting.

366
00:19:18,710 --> 00:19:20,621
The sunset up here is beautiful.

367
00:19:20,621 --> 00:19:23,564
I see it every day, and it never gets old.

368
00:19:26,930 --> 00:19:30,106
The sunset never looks the
same, even from the same spot.

369
00:19:30,198 --> 00:19:33,650
Sensei, that sounds nice, but
you're dodging the question.

370
00:19:33,716 --> 00:19:34,658
Let's toast!

371
00:19:34,686 --> 00:19:35,560
Cheers!

372
00:19:35,610 --> 00:19:37,846
A toast with melon bread? Really?

373
00:19:37,936 --> 00:19:39,936
Culturally speaking, it's acceptable.

374
00:19:40,029 --> 00:19:42,135
Owada, eat it properly.

375
00:19:42,286 --> 00:19:44,307
I am. Properly. Look.

376
00:19:44,359 --> 00:19:47,014
Today we got a good tomato.

377
00:19:47,132 --> 00:19:49,383
Red, small, and properly red.

378
00:19:49,463 --> 00:19:52,201
The roof is ours, for now!

379
00:19:52,582 --> 00:19:53,468
For now, yeah.

380
00:19:53,477 --> 00:19:55,266
For now is enough.

381
00:21:32,965 --> 00:21:35,739
Look what I found: a new
crop to experiment with.

382
00:21:38,480 --> 00:21:39,440
Wait, strawberries?

383
00:21:39,440 --> 00:21:41,508
Those are just runners, though.

384
00:21:41,508 --> 00:21:45,321
Runners can be multiplied, and
there's no limit to experiments.

385
00:21:45,443 --> 00:21:46,408
Wait.

386
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:47,955
Someone's going to pick the wrong pot.

387
00:21:47,955 --> 00:21:49,704
And then it's going to be a total mess.

388
00:21:49,756 --> 00:21:51,399
I'll do the picking!

389
00:21:51,459 --> 00:21:52,354
Oh, no, no.

390
00:21:52,465 --> 00:21:54,547
Next time, an experiment
that starts before dawn.

391
00:21:54,595 --> 00:21:56,390
Numbers don't lie, and they
don't wait for anyone.

392
00:21:56,469 --> 00:21:58,505
Continued on the rooftop, next time.

